From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 08:22:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 178/184] Sega CD: a door to the machine's own Backup RAM
 manager

This port lays a FAT12 volume over the whole of the backup memory, and
Sega's volume keeps its footer in the last 0x40 bytes, so a cartridge
can carry our files or a game's saves and not both. The way out is not
to write Sega's format ourselves; it is to stop writing over it.

The machine already has a Backup RAM file manager behind _BURAM at sub
0x5F16 -- BRMINIT, BRMSTAT, BRMSERCH, BRMREAD, BRMWRITE, BRMDEL,
BRMFORMAT, BRMDIR, BRMVERIFY. segacd_buram_call is the same visit to the
parked firmware that segacd_bios_call already makes to CDBIOS at 0x5F22,
three words along. Going through it keeps the directory layout, the
allocation and the free count as Sega's code's problem, permanently.

Two things about the visit are load-bearing and both are old lessons:

  Everything passed by pointer must live above 0x6000. The visit
  exchanges 0..0x6000; EmuTOS's stack is at 0x800, its .bss starts at
  0x2140, and segacd.o's own .bss runs 0x35F8..0x7FF2 -- straddling the
  line, so a static lands inside or outside by declaration order alone.
  That is how the printer's page buffer ended up at 0x2140. The work
  area, string buffer and call block go at 0x7E000 instead, inside the
  region already set aside for visits to the parked firmware, with an
  #error if the timeshare's read buffer ever grows into them.

  It must be called in supervisor mode. SCD_BRAM_INFO reaches an
  `ori.w #0x0700,sr`, and a .PRG calling through the cookie arrives in
  user mode. progs/cdtest.c documents this exact panic, sr=0010, about
  this exact mistake. It was made again here anyway.

SCD_BRAM_INFO (op 19) packs BRMINIT's status and size with BRMSTAT's
free and file count. It reads and does not write.

Measured, on a CD boot and a Mode 1 cartridge boot alike -- the Mode 1
loader parks the firmware too, so there is no boot path without this:

    raw 03027D00 -- Sega formatted, 2 blocks of 4096, 125 free, 0 files

and the volume byte-identical afterwards, footer intact, every byte
before it still zero.

BRMSTAT's unit is 0x40 bytes, not the 0x1000 megadev's header claims for
it: 125 against 8 KB is only true of 64-byte blocks, and Genesis Plus GX
computes the same 125 the same way. Two sources, one number; the header
is what is wrong. Noted in scdapi.h so the next reader does not have to
find it twice.

Nothing writes through this yet, and nothing should until it is known
whether a file's data can be altered in place under BRMSERCH's pointer.
docs/bram-filesystem.md has that question and the two after it.

SCD_VERSION 35.
